diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** No network-layer component is faulty. The complete diagnostic workflow — L2/L3 infrastructure sweep, duplicate-MAC scan, RIP routing-table inspection, traffic-control/qdisc audit, service/pressure sweep, link-history check, and exhaustive directed-IP ping tests — produced zero anomalies.

**Mechanism:** The Office segment (192.168.3.x: office_pc, enquiry, exam_cell, tpo) can reach every Internet Lab machine (128.168.0.x: pc2–pc5) by IP with 0% packet loss and round-trip times of 1–4 ms. Router1 correctly forwards Office traffic to router2 via the wan_r1_r2 link (RIP-learned route 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2), and router2 has the direct connected route back (192.168.3.0/24 via 20.20.0.1). Both directions are symmetrically healthy.

**Localization:** No device is implicated. All interfaces on router1 (the Office gateway) show zero errors, drops, and carrier events. No ACL, shaping rule, or flap mechanism exists anywhere on the path.

**Root cause:** There is no network fault. The live path from Office to Internet Lab is fully operational at the time of investigation.

**Fix:** No corrective network action is required. The incident should be escalated back to the reporting users to reproduce the issue with specific steps (application name, destination address, exact error message) so that any client-side or application-layer cause (e.g., missing hostname entries, a non-running service the users expected, or a now-cleared transient) can be investigated.
=== END ===